1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,720
Have you ever listened to a modern pop
song and thought: That's it? Four chords.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,570
A simple beat. A short melody.

3
00:00:07,408 --> 00:00:10,178
The same chorus repeated
over and over again.

4
00:00:10,948 --> 00:00:14,098
And yet somehow… it has
hundreds of millions of streams.

5
00:00:15,015 --> 00:00:18,115
Meanwhile, you might listen to
older music and hear complicated

6
00:00:18,195 --> 00:00:22,095
chord changes, long instrumental
sections, unexpected

7
00:00:22,175 --> 00:00:25,865
key changes, and melodies
that seem to go somewhere.

8
00:00:26,695 --> 00:00:31,125
So what happened? Did musicians
simply become less talented?

9
00:00:31,695 --> 00:00:34,275
Did streaming services
turn every song into

10
00:00:34,355 --> 00:00:36,525
a three-minute advertisement
for the chorus?

11
00:00:37,462 --> 00:00:39,722
Or is something more
interesting happening?

12
00:00:39,802 --> 00:00:42,622
Because modern music
may be simpler in some

13
00:00:42,702 --> 00:00:45,272
ways and far more complicated in others.

14
00:00:46,051 --> 00:00:47,581
And there's another problem.

15
00:00:48,311 --> 00:00:50,431
When we compare modern
music with music from the

16
00:00:50,511 --> 00:00:53,921
past, we may not actually
be comparing the same thing.

17
00:00:54,661 --> 00:00:57,511
Why does modern music
often sound so simple?

18
00:00:58,061 --> 00:01:01,811
And perhaps more importantly:
Is it actually simpler at all?

19
00:01:03,451 --> 00:01:06,301
First, we need to define
what we mean by simple.

20
00:01:06,931 --> 00:01:09,701
Because music can be
simple in one dimension

21
00:01:09,891 --> 00:01:12,161
and incredibly complicated in another.

22
00:01:12,935 --> 00:01:17,765
Take harmony. A huge number of popular
songs are built around just a few chords.

23
00:01:18,195 --> 00:01:20,325
Sometimes four. Sometimes three.

24
00:01:21,097 --> 00:01:25,007
The same chord progression may
repeat for almost the entire track.

25
00:01:25,757 --> 00:01:28,127
The melody might also
be relatively simple.

26
00:01:28,961 --> 00:01:31,271
Short phrases. Repeated patterns.

27
00:01:31,421 --> 00:01:35,651
And a chorus designed to get stuck
in your head after hearing it once.

28
00:01:36,547 --> 00:01:41,267
The rhythm is often predictable too.
A steady beat. A regular tempo. Verse.

29
00:01:41,347 --> 00:01:43,657
Chorus. Bridge. Final chorus.

30
00:01:44,536 --> 00:01:46,646
None of this means the song is bad.

31
00:01:47,416 --> 00:01:50,686
Writing something complicated
is relatively easy.

32
00:01:51,556 --> 00:01:55,446
Writing something simple that millions
of people remember is much harder.

33
00:01:56,220 --> 00:01:58,570
But there is another side to modern music.

34
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:00,930
The composition may be simple.

35
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:03,490
The production often isn't.

36
00:02:04,128 --> 00:02:06,848
A modern pop song can
contain dozens, or even

37
00:02:06,928 --> 00:02:09,718
hundreds, of individual sounds and layers.

38
00:02:11,724 --> 00:02:15,374
Synthesisers. Samples. Vocal
harmonies. Effects. Compression.

39
00:02:15,724 --> 00:02:18,954
Tiny sounds appearing for
only a fraction of a second.

40
00:02:19,767 --> 00:02:22,607
The listener may not
consciously notice most of them.

41
00:02:22,687 --> 00:02:24,497
But they are there.

42
00:02:24,847 --> 00:02:27,557
So perhaps the complexity
hasn't disappeared.

43
00:02:28,507 --> 00:02:29,467
Perhaps it has moved.

44
00:02:29,547 --> 00:02:33,137
From the notes themselves… to
the sound of the recording.

45
00:02:34,827 --> 00:02:36,917
Still, there is something real happening.

46
00:02:37,687 --> 00:02:39,377
And here's where it gets complicated.

47
00:02:40,527 --> 00:02:44,337
Some aspects of popular music have
become simpler, or more restricted.

48
00:02:45,295 --> 00:02:48,065
Certain harmonic patterns
have become less common,

49
00:02:48,175 --> 00:02:51,005
while others have become
increasingly familiar.

50
00:02:51,475 --> 00:02:55,185
But that doesn't mean music as
a whole became less diverse.

51
00:02:56,124 --> 00:03:00,334
In fact, some measures of musical
diversity tell a very different story.

52
00:03:01,144 --> 00:03:02,534
We'll come back to that.

53
00:03:03,862 --> 00:03:07,172
The famous four-chord progression
is almost a cliché at this point.

54
00:03:08,162 --> 00:03:12,972
You can hear similar harmonic patterns
across pop, rock, country and dance music.

55
00:03:13,837 --> 00:03:15,047
It sounds like this.

56
00:03:23,127 --> 00:03:25,957
Why? Well, one reason
is simple. They work.

57
00:03:26,907 --> 00:03:30,957
Certain chord progressions create a
satisfying feeling of tension and release.

58
00:03:31,801 --> 00:03:34,121
They are familiar. They
are emotionally flexible.

59
00:03:34,201 --> 00:03:38,051
And they leave plenty of room
for something else. The hook.

60
00:03:38,852 --> 00:03:41,102
Think of it like the
background of a painting.

61
00:03:42,052 --> 00:03:44,732
Nobody complains that the
sky is too simple if the

62
00:03:44,812 --> 00:03:47,042
important thing is
happening in the foreground.

63
00:03:49,062 --> 00:03:51,972
But technology has changed
something fundamental.

64
00:03:52,822 --> 00:03:54,572
Imagine buying an album
in nineteen seventy-five.

65
00:03:56,605 --> 00:03:59,075
You spend money on it. You take it home.

66
00:03:59,545 --> 00:04:02,455
You are probably willing to
give the music some time.

67
00:04:03,189 --> 00:04:05,939
Maybe the interesting part
starts after a minute.

68
00:04:06,769 --> 00:04:10,799
Maybe the strange intro only makes
sense when you hear the entire album.

69
00:04:11,543 --> 00:04:13,493
Now imagine a streaming service.

70
00:04:14,403 --> 00:04:15,743
You have access to millions of songs.

71
00:04:15,823 --> 00:04:20,173
And if you don't like what you
hear… you can skip. Immediately.

72
00:04:21,179 --> 00:04:22,679
There is almost no cost.

73
00:04:22,759 --> 00:04:24,969
And that changes the incentives.

74
00:04:25,939 --> 00:04:30,209
A song now has to compete with virtually
every other song ever recorded.

75
00:04:31,092 --> 00:04:34,022
The first few seconds
matter. A memorable sound.

76
00:04:34,312 --> 00:04:36,252
A recognisable voice. A beat.

77
00:04:36,332 --> 00:04:38,322
Something that says: Don't skip me.

78
00:04:38,996 --> 00:04:43,366
And this is one reason why modern
songs often get to the point faster.

79
00:04:44,336 --> 00:04:47,466
Long instrumental introductions
have become less common.

80
00:04:48,182 --> 00:04:51,062
Researchers measured more than
three hundred American top ten

81
00:04:51,142 --> 00:04:54,592
singles, released between nineteen
eighty-six and twenty fifteen.

82
00:04:56,022 --> 00:04:57,902
The wait before the first vocal had fallen

83
00:04:57,982 --> 00:05:02,952
from over twenty seconds to about five
seconds. A drop of nearly eighty per cent.

84
00:05:04,675 --> 00:05:08,205
And over the same period, the average
tempo rose by about eight per cent.

85
00:05:10,548 --> 00:05:13,238
The same researchers
also measured the titles.

86
00:05:14,088 --> 00:05:15,538
Song names got shorter.

87
00:05:15,788 --> 00:05:18,378
More and more of them are a single word.

88
00:05:19,096 --> 00:05:23,246
And in pop, the title is usually the
hook — the line you actually sing.

89
00:05:24,156 --> 00:05:27,986
After the vocals start, it now arrives
about eighteen per cent sooner.

90
00:05:28,751 --> 00:05:29,991
And there is something else.

91
00:05:30,071 --> 00:05:34,001
Sixty years ago, an instrumental
could top the charts.

92
00:05:34,831 --> 00:05:36,641
Today that almost never happens.

93
00:05:37,513 --> 00:05:39,743
Almost everything in the charts is sung.

94
00:05:40,713 --> 00:05:45,223
And the singing is about anything at all
— love, money, grief, a Tuesday afternoon.

95
00:05:45,995 --> 00:05:48,865
The song is not necessarily
trying to be lazy.

96
00:05:49,795 --> 00:05:53,725
It is competing in an environment where
the listener can leave at any moment.

97
00:05:54,498 --> 00:05:57,848
And now music exists inside
an enormous system of data.

98
00:05:58,878 --> 00:06:01,648
Streaming platforms can
measure what people skip,

99
00:06:01,818 --> 00:06:04,288
what they replay, and
where they stop listening.

100
00:06:05,071 --> 00:06:08,891
This doesn't mean an algorithm
tells musicians which chords to use.

101
00:06:08,971 --> 00:06:11,561
But data changes the environment.

102
00:06:12,391 --> 00:06:15,161
This is not a conspiracy. It's evolution.

103
00:06:16,821 --> 00:06:19,271
Then social media added something else.

104
00:06:20,101 --> 00:06:24,291
Sometimes a song doesn't become popular
because people love the whole song.

105
00:06:25,121 --> 00:06:27,831
Sometimes they love fifteen seconds of it.

106
00:06:28,505 --> 00:06:32,095
One particular line. One
drop. One strange sound.

107
00:06:32,765 --> 00:06:35,735
One moment that works
perfectly in a short video.

108
00:06:36,497 --> 00:06:38,557
If one section can
spread across millions of

109
00:06:38,637 --> 00:06:41,907
videos, that section
becomes incredibly valuable.

110
00:06:42,777 --> 00:06:44,867
Almost like a tiny musical logo.

111
00:06:46,837 --> 00:06:49,267
Now we come to the most important problem.

112
00:06:50,117 --> 00:06:53,227
Was old music actually
better? Or more complicated?

113
00:06:54,359 --> 00:06:57,369
Well… we have a very serious
case of survivorship bias.

114
00:06:58,179 --> 00:07:02,389
When we think about the music of the
nineteen sixties, we remember The Beatles.

115
00:07:03,331 --> 00:07:06,701
The Rolling Stones. Pink
Floyd. Led Zeppelin.

116
00:07:07,291 --> 00:07:11,561
But those decades also produced
thousands of songs that nobody remembers.

117
00:07:12,517 --> 00:07:14,767
Simple songs. Repetitive songs.

118
00:07:14,957 --> 00:07:16,097
Terrible songs.

119
00:07:16,177 --> 00:07:19,847
Songs that were probably
annoying even when they were new.

120
00:07:20,551 --> 00:07:22,041
They simply disappeared.

121
00:07:22,751 --> 00:07:24,381
Time acted like a filter.

122
00:07:25,411 --> 00:07:29,361
The music that survives tends to be the
music that people continued to value.

123
00:07:30,187 --> 00:07:34,337
So when we compare today's charts
with the past, we are often comparing:

124
00:07:34,847 --> 00:07:38,957
the average music of today with the
best music that survived sixty years.

125
00:07:39,755 --> 00:07:41,945
That's not exactly a fair fight.

126
00:07:42,775 --> 00:07:44,995
Imagine someone from the
year twenty seventy-six

127
00:07:45,075 --> 00:07:48,265
listening only to the
greatest songs from our time.

128
00:07:49,027 --> 00:07:53,817
They may conclude that the twenty-twenties
were an incredible golden age of music.

129
00:07:54,427 --> 00:07:58,537
They might never even discover half of
the songs we currently complain about.

130
00:08:00,417 --> 00:08:03,047
And complicated music never disappeared.

131
00:08:03,897 --> 00:08:06,207
It may just not be sitting
at the top of the charts.

132
00:08:07,213 --> 00:08:10,693
When researchers analysed the chords
and textures of fifty years of

133
00:08:10,773 --> 00:08:15,263
American chart music, they found that
variety did not steadily decline.

134
00:08:16,141 --> 00:08:19,001
It fell to a low point in
the mid-eighties, and then

135
00:08:19,081 --> 00:08:21,351
rose again — to a peak in
the early two-thousands.

136
00:08:23,936 --> 00:08:27,166
In classical music, complexity
often lived in harmony.

137
00:08:28,176 --> 00:08:30,866
In progressive rock, in
unusual time signatures.

138
00:08:31,956 --> 00:08:35,786
In modern electronic music, it may
live somewhere completely different.

139
00:08:36,644 --> 00:08:38,894
Sound design. Rhythmic layering.

140
00:08:39,284 --> 00:08:40,964
The construction of a beat.

141
00:08:41,044 --> 00:08:42,994
The manipulation of a human voice.

142
00:08:43,889 --> 00:08:47,939
A producer might spend hours creating
a sound that lasts two seconds.

143
00:08:48,909 --> 00:08:51,239
The final song may use only four chords.

144
00:08:52,072 --> 00:08:55,382
And this kind of craft is no
longer locked inside a studio.

145
00:08:55,832 --> 00:08:59,002
Today, almost anyone can make music.

146
00:08:59,752 --> 00:09:02,882
A computer can contain an
entire virtual orchestra.

147
00:09:03,777 --> 00:09:07,307
A studio that once filled a
room now fits inside a laptop.

148
00:09:08,197 --> 00:09:10,447
Anyone with a computer can make a record.

149
00:09:11,406 --> 00:09:13,846
And that is one reason
why a single streaming

150
00:09:13,926 --> 00:09:16,956
service now carries over
a hundred million tracks.

151
00:09:17,838 --> 00:09:21,768
And enough software to keep you busy
until the heat death of the universe.

152
00:09:22,649 --> 00:09:26,179
But much of that software already
contains accumulated knowledge.

153
00:09:27,009 --> 00:09:30,109
Presets, drum samples,
mastering chains — decades

154
00:09:30,189 --> 00:09:33,019
of studio techniques, packaged into tools.

155
00:09:33,883 --> 00:09:37,113
So the songwriter no longer has
to carry all the complexity.

156
00:09:37,903 --> 00:09:42,053
The song can be simple because the
sound around it doesn't have to be.

157
00:09:42,813 --> 00:09:45,023
Those same four chords, on their own.

158
00:09:51,873 --> 00:09:53,743
And the same four chords, produced.

159
00:10:01,033 --> 00:10:04,823
It's a bit like comparing an old
mechanical watch with a modern smartphone.

160
00:10:05,553 --> 00:10:08,083
The watch may be mechanically fascinating.

161
00:10:09,045 --> 00:10:11,915
The smartphone may look like
a simple black rectangle.

162
00:10:12,325 --> 00:10:16,895
But simplicity on the surface doesn't
necessarily mean simplicity underneath.

163
00:10:18,845 --> 00:10:23,355
Perhaps the final answer is much older
than streaming or artificial intelligence.

164
00:10:24,185 --> 00:10:28,155
Humans like patterns. We like
familiarity. We like repetition.

165
00:10:29,203 --> 00:10:33,453
When the brain can predict what comes
next, it receives a small reward.

166
00:10:34,403 --> 00:10:37,553
But if the music is completely
predictable, it becomes boring.

167
00:10:38,474 --> 00:10:42,304
So successful music often lives
in a very interesting zone.

168
00:10:42,994 --> 00:10:44,744
Familiar enough to understand.

169
00:10:45,634 --> 00:10:47,204
Different enough to remain interesting.

170
00:10:48,174 --> 00:10:50,724
So… why is modern music so simple?

171
00:10:51,434 --> 00:10:54,114
Partly because streaming
rewards immediacy, and

172
00:10:54,194 --> 00:10:56,864
partly because simple
music has always worked.

173
00:10:57,637 --> 00:11:00,787
But perhaps mainstream music
isn't becoming simpler.

174
00:11:01,577 --> 00:11:03,887
Perhaps it is becoming more optimised.

175
00:11:05,267 --> 00:11:08,127
Optimised for attention.
Optimised for memorability.

176
00:11:08,207 --> 00:11:10,117
Optimised for replay.

177
00:11:10,447 --> 00:11:13,047
And optimised for
surviving in a world where

178
00:11:13,127 --> 00:11:15,937
millions of other songs
are only one click away.

179
00:11:16,713 --> 00:11:19,773
Meanwhile, somewhere on the
internet, someone is probably

180
00:11:19,853 --> 00:11:23,263
writing a seventeen-minute
song in eleven-eight time…

181
00:11:23,513 --> 00:11:26,423
and putting it online for
twelve people to discover.

182
00:11:27,199 --> 00:11:29,709
And honestly? That's probably fine too.

183
00:11:29,819 --> 00:11:32,729
Because music has never
been just one thing.

184
00:11:33,441 --> 00:11:37,111
The real question may not be:
"Why is modern music so simple?"

185
00:11:37,721 --> 00:11:41,011
Maybe the better question is:
"Simple compared to what?"

186
00:11:42,751 --> 00:11:45,791
Because if we compare today's
average pop song with the greatest

187
00:11:45,871 --> 00:11:50,521
music of the past, modern music
can look disappointingly simple.

188
00:11:51,273 --> 00:11:54,453
But if we compare average
music from every era… we

189
00:11:54,533 --> 00:11:56,883
might discover something
slightly uncomfortable.

190
00:11:57,855 --> 00:12:01,565
Maybe people have always been
writing catchy, repetitive songs.

191
00:12:02,115 --> 00:12:04,125
We just forgot most of them.
